course: dictionary
lesson: 32
title: What is an abuser?
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: An abuser is a person who hurts, frightens or treats another person, or an animal, badly, often again and again. The word also has a second meaning: someone who uses something in a harmful or wrong way, such as drugs, alcohol or their power.
explain: «After England lost the Euro 2020 final on penalties, three players who missed were racially abused online, and some of the abusers were prosecuted.» The abusers are the people who sent the cruel messages. The sentence tells you they did harm, because the players were the ones who were hurt.
explain: Abuse is never the fault of the person who is hurt. If someone is hurting or frightening you, tell a trusted adult, such as a parent, carer or teacher, straight away.
fact: An abuser is a person who hurts, frightens or treats someone or an animal badly.
fact: An abuser can also be someone who uses drugs, alcohol or power in a harmful or wrong way.
fact: «Prosecuted» means taken to court because of a crime.
fact: With a hateful post online, the right move is to report it, not share it.
fact: Harm is never the child's fault, and a trusted adult can always help.
exercise: What is an abuser? | A person who plays football for their country | A person who makes a loud noise | A teacher who marks homework | *A person who hurts or frightens another person, often again and again
exercise: «Some of the abusers were prosecuted.» What does prosecuted mean here? | *Taken to court because of a crime | Given a prize for winning | Cheered loudly by the crowd | Sent home early from school
exercise: Someone online is sending you cruel messages. What should you do? | *Tell a trusted adult straight away | Wait and hope it stops by itself | Share the messages with your friends | Reply with something even crueller
test: The second meaning of abuser is someone who uses something in a harmful or wrong way. Which example fits best? | A dog who sleeps all day in the sun | *Someone who uses their power to make others afraid | A child who reads a book in the library | Someone who gives a present to a friend
test: Why is the person who is abused never to blame? | Because they should have been stronger | Because they must have said something wrong | Because adults always decide who is guilty | *Because harm is never the child's own fault
exam: A hateful post appears about someone you know. What is the right move? | Reply with a mean comment | Share it so everyone can see it | Save a copy and show it to your friends | *Report it and do not share it, then tell a trusted adult
